|~ What is declared under `::any`, the kind beneath every kind: the
   verbs every value answers. A word several kinds answer is declared
   under each of them, and a call whose subject no kind of its walk
   answers is refused with `:addresses`, where the word lives. ~|

:any/JsonValueNotEncodableError
  |~~ JSON has no form for a value a host put in the scope that is of
      none of the kinds of qlang: `json` writes such a value that runs as
      `<host-fn name>` and refuses any other, so a query projects around
      it, `/name`, before it writes JSON.
  ~~|

:any/desc
  |~~ The subject turned to descend, a value of `::desc`: a key of
      `sort`, `min` or `max`, or a place of a vector key, that ranks the
      greater first, whatever the kind of what it ranks.
  ~~|
  ::builtin{}

:any/desc/descendsASort
  |~~ A sort by `desc` descends. ~~|
  ::law~([1 3 2] | sort ~(desc) | eq [3 2 1])

:any/desc/turnsAMaximum
  |~~ A descending key turns the maximum into the minimum. ~~|
  ::law~([{:n 1} {:n 2}] | max ~(/n | desc) | eq {:n 1})

:any/desc/answersADesc
  |~~ The answer is a value of `::desc`. ~~|
  ::law~(5 | desc | type | eq ::desc)

:any/desc/descending
  |~~ The value turned to descend. ~~|
  ::desc

:any/eq
  |~~ Whether the subject equals the value, by structural equality deep
      across maps, vectors, sets and scalars.
  ~~|
  ::builtin{}

:any/eq/holdsOfANumberAndItself
  |~~ A number equals itself. ~~|
  ::law~(42 | eq 42 | eq true)

:any/eq/comparesMapsByStructure
  |~~ Two maps of the same entries are equal. ~~|
  ::law~({:a 1} | eq {:a 1} | eq true)

:any/eq/value
  |~~ The value the call gives. ~~|
  ~(/0)

:any/eq/same
  |~~ Whether the two values are equal. ~~|
  ::boolean

:any/ne
  |~~ Whether the subject differs from the value, by the structural
      equality of `eq`, whose negation it is.
  ~~|
  ::builtin{}

:any/ne/holdsOfTwoNumbers
  |~~ One differs from two. ~~|
  ::law~(1 | ne 2 | eq true)

:any/ne/failsOfEqualMaps
  |~~ Two maps of the same entries do not differ. ~~|
  ::law~({:a 1} | ne {:a 1} | eq false)

:any/ne/value
  |~~ The value the call gives. ~~|
  ~(/0)

:any/ne/different
  |~~ Whether the two values differ. ~~|
  ::boolean

:any/type
  |~~ The value's kind, a tag, which names the page of the value and the
      verbs it answers: `type | doc` reads the kind's page, and asking
      what a value is composes `type` with `eq`, `filter ~(type | eq
      ::string)`. A kind of the core is named under the prefix `qlang/`
      and written short.

      The literal of a value of the core implies its kind.
      A binding record, a descriptor of the catalog, a quote and a tagged
      value answer the tag they carry.
      An error answers the tag of its site, which `!|` keeps on the
      descriptor it opens.
  ~~|
  ::builtin{}

:any/type/ofANumber
  |~~ A number literal is a number. ~~|
  ::law~(42 | type | eq ::number)

:any/type/ofAString
  |~~ A string literal is a string. ~~|
  ::law~("hi" | type | eq ::string)

:any/type/ofAKeyword
  |~~ A keyword literal is a keyword. ~~|
  ::law~(:foo | type | eq ::keyword)

:any/type/ofAVector
  |~~ A vector literal is a vector. ~~|
  ::law~([1 2] | type | eq ::vec)

:any/type/ofASet
  |~~ A set literal is a set. ~~|
  ::law~(#[1 2] | type | eq ::set)

:any/type/ofAQuote
  |~~ A quote answers `::quote`. ~~|
  ::law~(~(42) | type | eq ::quote)

:any/type/ofAnError
  |~~ An error answers the tag of the refusal that raised it. ~~|
  ::law~("hello" | add 1 !| [type /actualType] | eq [::number/add/SubjectNotNumberError ::string])

:any/type/kind
  |~~ The kind of the value, its outermost tag. ~~|
  ::tag

:any/json
  |~~ The subject rendered as a JSON string. The shapes of qlang alone
      lose their identity at this boundary: a keyword becomes its bare
      name, a key and a value alike (`:foo` → `"foo"`), a set flattens
      to an array, and a map writes its keys as the keys of an object.
      It hands values to consumers that do not read qlang.
  ~~|
  ::builtin{}

:any/json/writesAMapAsAnObject
  |~~ A map is written as an object. ~~|
  ::law~({:a 1 :b 2} | json | eq "{\"a\":1,\"b\":2}")

:any/json/writesKeywordsAsNames
  |~~ A keyword is written as its bare name. ~~|
  ::law~([:foo :bar] | json | eq "[\"foo\",\"bar\"]")

:any/json/writesASetAsAnArray
  |~~ A set is written as an array. ~~|
  ::law~(#[1 2 3] | json | eq "[1,2,3]")

:any/json/text
  |~~ The JSON text of the value. ~~|
  ::string

:any/print
  |~~ The text that reads back as the subject, which the command line
      shows for an answer: an atom as its literal, a vector and a map by
      their brackets around the print of each part, the set, the quote,
      the doc and the error by their own brackets, and any other value
      under a tag as its tag before the print of its payload. A kind may
      answer a `print` of its own declared under it, `:Box/print`, and the
      print of a value takes it for each part of that kind.
  ~~|
  ::builtin{}

:any/print/printsANumber
  |~~ A number prints as its literal. ~~|
  ::law~(42 | print | eq "42")

:any/print/printsASetInItsOrder
  |~~ A set prints in its one order. ~~|
  ::law~(#[2 1] | print | eq "#[1 2]")

:any/print/printsATaggedValue
  |~~ A tagged value prints with its tag. ~~|
  ::law~(::builtin{:a 1} | print | eq "::builtin{:a 1}")

:any/print/readsBack
  |~~ The text reads back as the value. ~~|
  ::law~({:a [1 "x"]} | :v / | print | parse | :q / | apply q | eq v)

:any/print/literal
  |~~ The literal the value prints as, a string. ~~|
  ::string

:any/elide
  |~~ The value within a budget of characters of its print, what does
      not fit given way to `::elision` markers: the largest part
      beneath the top gives way first, a vector keeping the first half of
      its elements and ending with a marker of the rest, a string or a map
      giving way whole. A marker's `:read` applied to the value `elide`
      took answers the part left out, and a value whose print fits is
      answered as it is. The command line applies it to every answer
      it prints when `--budget=N` asks it to.
  ~~|
  ::builtin{}

:any/elide/leavesWhatFits
  |~~ A value within the budget stays whole. ~~|
  ::law~([1 2] | elide 100 | eq [1 2])

:any/elide/elidesTheLargestPart
  |~~ The largest part gives way to an elision. ~~|
  ::law~({:s "a long line of text to leave out" :n 1} | elide 40 | /s | type | eq ::elision)

:any/elide/readsTheElidedPartBack
  |~~ An elision holds the query that reads its part back. ~~|
  ::law~({:s "a long line of text to leave out" :n 1} | :v / | elide 40 | /s/read | :r / | v | apply r | eq (v | /s))

:any/elide/budget
  |~~ The budget, in characters of the print. ~~|
  ~(/0)

:any/elide/budget/NotNumberError
  |~~ The budget is a number. ~~|
  ::holds~(is ::number)

:any/if
  |~~ The branch the condition chooses, run against the subject: `if
      cond ~(then) ~(else)`. The condition is a boolean computed at the
      call, each branch a quote, and only the chosen branch runs.

      A condition that is not a boolean is refused, since it has no
      second reading. The empty quote `~()` is the branch that
      leaves the subject as it is.
  ~~|
  ::builtin{}

:any/if/runsThenWhenItHolds
  |~~ The then branch runs when the condition holds. ~~|
  ::law~(5 | if (gt 0) ~("positive") ~("non-positive") | eq "positive")

:any/if/runsElseWhenItFails
  |~~ The else branch runs when it does not. ~~|
  ::law~(-1 | if (gt 0) ~("positive") ~("non-positive") | eq "non-positive")

:any/if/readsTheSubject
  |~~ The condition reads the subject. ~~|
  ::law~({:age 25} | if (/age | gte 18) ~("adult") ~("minor") | eq "adult")

:any/if/runsTheBranchOnTheSubject
  |~~ A branch runs against the subject. ~~|
  ::law~(5 | if (gt 0) ~(add 100) ~() | eq 105)

:any/if/condition
  |~~ The condition, a boolean.

      The condition of `if` answers a boolean; null, a number, a string
      or any other value is refused where it stands.
  ~~|
  ~(/0)

:any/if/condition/refusesANumber
  |~~ A number condition is refused. ~~|
  ::law~(5 | if 1 ~("yes") ~("no") !| [type /actualType] | eq [::any/if/condition/NotBooleanError ::number])

:any/if/condition/NotBooleanError
  |~~ The condition is a boolean. ~~|
  ::holds~(is ::boolean)

:any/if/then
  |~~ The branch run when the condition holds.

      The then branch of `if` is code it runs only when chosen, so it
      takes a quote, `if (gt 0) ~("yes") ~("no")`; every branch is checked at the call.
  ~~|
  ~(/1)

:any/if/then/refusesAString
  |~~ A string given as the then branch is refused. ~~|
  ::law~(5 | if true "yes" ~("no") !| [type /actualType] | eq [::any/if/then/NotQuoteError ::string])

:any/if/then/NotQuoteError
  |~~ The then is code, a quote. ~~|
  ::holds~(is ::quote)

:any/if/else
  |~~ The branch run when it does not.

      The else branch of `if` is code it runs only when chosen, so it
      takes a quote, `if (gt 0) ~("yes") ~("no")`; every branch is checked at the call.
  ~~|
  ~(/2)

:any/if/else/refusesAString
  |~~ A string given as the else branch is refused. ~~|
  ::law~(5 | if true ~("yes") "no" !| [type /actualType] | eq [::any/if/else/NotQuoteError ::string])

:any/if/else/NotQuoteError
  |~~ The else is code, a quote. ~~|
  ::holds~(is ::quote)

:any/coalesce
  |~~ The first answer of the alternatives, run in order against the
      subject, that is neither null nor an error; every other value,
      false, 0, "", [], {} and #[] among them, ends the walk, and a walk
      that skips them all answers null. `coalesce ~(/key) ~(fallback)`
      reads "the key where the map holds it, else the fallback": `/key`
      answers an error on a map without the key, which the walk skips as
      it skips null. An alternative that is no quote answers itself, and a
      call of such alternatives alone puts the subject first, so
      `coalesce 30` reads "the subject, else 30".
  ~~|
  ::builtin{}

:any/coalesce/passesOverNull
  |~~ A null answer passes to the next alternative. ~~|
  ::law~({:nickname null :name "alice"} | coalesce ~(/nickname) ~(/name) | eq "alice")

:any/coalesce/answersTheFirstValue
  |~~ The first answer that is a value wins. ~~|
  ::law~({:nickname "ace" :name "alice"} | coalesce ~(/nickname) ~(/name) | eq "ace")

:any/coalesce/passesOverAnError
  |~~ An error passes to the next alternative. ~~|
  ::law~({:a 2 :b 3} | coalesce ~(/missing) ~("fallback") | eq "fallback")

:any/coalesce/fallsToTheLast
  |~~ The last alternative answers when the rest give nothing. ~~|
  ::law~({:nickname null :name null} | coalesce ~(/nickname) ~(/name) ~("anonymous") | eq "anonymous")

:any/coalesce/defaultsAMissingField
  |~~ A field missing or null takes the default. ~~|
  ::law~([{:t 45} {:t null} {}] * (at :t | coalesce 30) | eq [45 30 30])

:any/coalesce/keepsAValue
  |~~ A value present stays. ~~|
  ::law~({:tags ["a"]} | /tags | coalesce [] | eq ["a"])

:any/coalesce/takesAValueForAnAlternative
  |~~ A value stands for an alternative as its own answer. ~~|
  ::law~({:a 2} | coalesce ~(/missing) "fallback" | eq "fallback")

:any/coalesce/alternatives
  |~~ The alternatives, in their order. ~~|
  ~(/)

:any/coalesce/alternatives/EmptyError
  |~~ `coalesce` answers the first of its alternatives that answers a
      value, so it takes one at least, `coalesce ~(/nickname) ~("anonymous")`.
  ~~|
  ::holds~(count | gt 0)

:any/coalesce/alternatives/EmptyError/refusesNoAlternative
  |~~ A coalesce of no alternative is refused. ~~|
  ::law~({} | coalesce !| [type /actualValue] | eq [::any/coalesce/alternatives/EmptyError ::any/coalesce[]])

:any/cond
  |~~ The branch of the first predicate that answers true against the
      subject, the clauses alternating predicate and branch, each
      predicate answering a boolean. A clause left over after the pairs
      is the default branch; without one, a walk that matches nothing
      answers null. It flattens a chain of nested `if`.
  ~~|
  ::builtin{}

:any/cond/picksTheFirstThatHolds
  |~~ Eighty-five is a B. ~~|
  ::law~(85 | cond ~(gte 90) ~("A") ~(gte 80) ~("B") ~(gte 70) ~("C") ~("F") | eq "B")

:any/cond/fallsToTheDefault
  |~~ A score no predicate holds of takes the default. ~~|
  ::law~(65 | cond ~(gte 90) ~("A") ~(gte 80) ~("B") ~(gte 70) ~("C") ~("F") | eq "F")

:any/cond/readsTheSubject
  |~~ The predicates read the subject. ~~|
  ::law~({:role "admin"} | cond ~(/role | eq "admin") ~("full") ~(/role | eq "user") ~("limited") | eq "full")

:any/cond/clauses
  |~~ The clauses, each a condition and its branch. ~~|
  ~(/)

:any/cond/clauses/ElementNotQuoteError
  |~~ Each of the clauses is code, a quote. ~~|
  ::holds~(every ~(is ::quote))

:any/cond/clauses/NoBranchesError
  |~~ `cond` takes its clauses in pairs, a predicate and its branch, a
      last clause alone the default; a call without one pair, a lone
      default among them, has nothing to answer.
  ~~|
  ::holds~(count | gte 2)

:any/cond/clauses/NoBranchesError/refusesNoPair
  |~~ A cond of no pair is refused. ~~|
  ::law~(5 | cond !| type | eq ::any/cond/clauses/NoBranchesError)

:any/cond/ConditionNotBooleanError
  |~~ Every predicate of `cond` answers a boolean; `:index` is the place
      in the args, from zero, of the predicate that answered another
      kind.
  ~~|

:any/cond/ConditionNotBooleanError/refusesANumberAnswer
  |~~ A predicate that answers a number is refused at its place. ~~|
  ::law~(5 | cond ~(1) ~("yes") !| [type /index /actualType] | eq [::any/cond/ConditionNotBooleanError 0 ::number])

:any/apply
  |~~ The value of the code run against the subject, under the fork
      rule: the declarations the code makes stay inside it, and only its
      value comes out. The subject comes first, as for every verb, so
      `x | apply /` runs a quote held as the subject against itself, and
      `"src" | parse | apply /` evaluates source text.

      The quote's first step rides `|` against the subject unless the
      quote carries a leading combinator (`*`, `!|`), which routes it
      through that combinator, so the pipeline suffix a `:trail` records
      runs again as it is.
  ~~|
  ::builtin{}

:any/apply/runsTheCode
  |~~ The code runs against the subject. ~~|
  ::law~(42 | apply ~(type | eq ::null) | eq false)

:any/apply/runsADistribute
  |~~ A distribute runs over the subject. ~~|
  ::law~([1 2 3] | apply ~(* mul 2) | eq [2 4 6])

:any/apply/runsStepsInOrder
  |~~ Several steps run in order. ~~|
  ::law~([[1 2] [3 4]] | apply ~(flat | sort) | eq [1 2 3 4])

:any/apply/runsParsedText
  |~~ Text parsed into a quote runs. ~~|
  ::law~("10 | add 3" | parse | apply / | eq 13)

:any/apply/code
  |~~ The code the call runs.

      The code `apply` runs on its subject is a quote, `apply ~(add 1)`,
      or a name that holds one; a value computed at the call is refused,
      the error a step answered there among them.
  ~~|
  ~(/0)

:any/apply/code/refusesANumber
  |~~ A number computed at the call is refused. ~~|
  ::law~(42 | apply 1 !| [type /actualType] | eq [::any/apply/code/NotQuoteError ::number])

:any/apply/code/NotQuoteError
  |~~ The code is code, a quote. ~~|
  ::holds~(is ::quote)

:any/env
  |~~ What the reader declared, where the step stands: a map from each
      name the cells of a session and the steps before it declared, one
      with a path among them, to the record of its binding, `::binding`
      with its name, docs, value, source and origin, a fork's own
      declarations among them inside the fork. What the language and the
      host declared stands apart: `kinds` lists their kinds, and
      `manifest` what lies under a noun. `env | /flag` hands one record
      to the next step.
  ~~|
  ::builtin{}

:any/env/holdsADeclaredName
  |~~ A name declared before the step is held. ~~|
  ::law~(:limit 10 | env | has :limit)

:any/env/isEmptyAtTheStart
  |~~ Nothing is declared at the start. ~~|
  ::law~(env | eq {})

:any/env/holdsANameWithAPath
  |~~ A name with a path is held by its address. ~~|
  ::law~(:Box |~~ A box. ~~| | :Box/area ~(1) | env | keys | eq #[:Box :Box/area])

:any/env/holdsTheRecord
  |~~ Each name holds the record its declaration wrote. ~~|
  ::law~(42 | :answer / | env | /answer | /value | eq 42)

:any/env/names
  |~~ The records of what the reader declared, by name. ~~|
  ::map

:any/kinds
  |~~ The kinds the scope reads, each by its tag, the labels of one
      segment, a topic among them: those the language and the host
      declared, then the reader's, so a query asks which kinds there are
      before it asks one of them for its page, `::set | doc`, or for what
      lies under it, `::set | manifest`.
  ~~|
  ::builtin{}

:any/kinds/holdsTheKindsOfTheLanguage
  |~~ The kinds of the language are read. ~~|
  ::law~(kinds | has ::vec)

:any/kinds/holdsAReadersKind
  |~~ A kind the reader declares is read. ~~|
  ::law~(:Box |~~ A box. ~~| | kinds | has ::Box)

:any/kinds/holdsTheTopic
  |~~ The topic of the language is a label too. ~~|
  ::law~(kinds | has ::qlang)

:any/kinds/labels
  |~~ The kinds the scope reads. ~~|
  ::set

:any/binding
  |~~ The record of a name, what a declaration wrote into its scope:
      `::binding` with its `:name`, its `:docs`, its `:value`, its
      `:source` and its `:origin`, the source it was read from, so where
      a name was declared is read as data. The other axes project it:
      `doc` its page, `source` its quote. A keyword
      after it reads the record of the member the subject holds under it.

      A verb by its address reads the record its source declared.
      A name of the scope reads the record its declaration wrote.
      Any other value reads the record of its kind.
  ~~|
  ::builtin{}

:any/binding/readsTheOriginOfAVerb
  |~~ A verb's record names the source that declared it. ~~|
  ::law~(::vec/filter | binding | /origin | eq :qlang/vec)

:any/binding/answersABinding
  |~~ The record is a `::binding`. ~~|
  ::law~(::vec/filter | binding | type | eq ::binding)

:any/binding/readsTheValueOfAName
  |~~ A name's record holds its value. ~~|
  ::law~(:x 1 | :x | binding | /value | eq 1)

:any/binding/readsTheKindOfAValue
  |~~ A number reads the record of `::number`. ~~|
  ::law~(5 | binding | /name | eq :number)

:any/binding/anchor
  |~~ The anchor within the page, null when it gives none. ~~|
  ~(at 0)

:any/binding/anchor/NotKeywordOrNullError
  |~~ The anchor is a keyword or null. ~~|
  ::holds~(is ::keyword ::null)

:any/binding/record
  |~~ The record of the name. ~~|
  ::binding

:any/binding/UnknownNameError
  |~~ `binding` found no declaration of the name it read: a keyword that
      names no binding of its scope, the last name of a verb declared
      under a kind among them, or a tag name that names nothing.
      `:name` holds the name, and `:addresses` the addresses of
      the pages of that name, one for each kind that answers it. `source`, `doc`,
      `explain` and `runLaws` read a name as `binding` does, and
      each is refused under a tag of its own, so the tag says which of
      them read it.
  ~~|

:any/binding/UnknownNameError/refusesANameNothingDeclares
  |~~ A name nothing declares is refused. ~~|
  ::law~(:nonexistent-binding | binding !| [type /name] | eq [::any/binding/UnknownNameError :nonexistent-binding])

:any/binding/UnknownNameError/name
  |~~ The name the axis read, a keyword or a tag. ~~|
  #[::keyword ::tag]

:any/binding/UnknownNameError/addresses
  |~~ The addresses of the pages of that name, one for each kind that answers it. ~~|
  ::set

:any/binding/UnknownNameError/nearest
  |~~ The names nearest to it in spelling. ~~|
  ::vec

:any/source
  |~~ The code written under a name: the declaration that binds it in
      its scope and every declaration under its address, its parts, its
      answer, its refusals and its laws, one quote of their steps in the
      order they were written, which `parse` prints as its text;
      `::Tag | source` what was written under the tag. A binding no step
      declared, a value a host bound, answers null.
      Any other value reads the declaration of its kind. Given a name,
      it reads the name the subject holds under it: for a tag name the
      name under its address, a part of the args of a word among them,
      and for any other subject the word the name calls after it.
  ~~|
  ::builtin{}

:any/source/printsTheDeclaration
  |~~ The source of a verb prints as its declaration. ~~|
  ::law~(::vec/filter | source | parse | contains ":vec/filter")

:any/source/printsARefusal
  |~~ A refusal's source prints as its declaration. ~~|
  ::law~(::number/NotFiniteError | source | parse | contains "NotFiniteError")

:any/source/readsTheKindOfAValue
  |~~ A string reads the source of `::string`. ~~|
  ::law~("text" | source | eq (::string | source))

:any/source/readsAPartByAnAnchor
  |~~ An anchor reads the source of a part. ~~|
  ::law~(::vec/take | source :count | eq (::vec/take/count | source))

:any/source/readsEveryDeclarationUnderAName
  |~~ The source of a word holds the declarations of its parts. ~~|
  ::law~(::vec/take | source | * /name | has :vec/take/count)

:any/source/name
  |~~ The name the call gives, null when it gives none. ~~|
  ~(at 0)

:any/source/name/NotKeywordOrNullError
  |~~ The name is a keyword or null. ~~|
  ::holds~(is ::keyword ::null)

:any/source/code
  |~~ The code written under the name, null for a value a host bound. ~~|
  #[::quote ::null]

:any/source/UnknownNameError
  |~~ `source` found no declaration of the name it read, as `binding`
      finds none.
      ::link~(::any/binding/UnknownNameError | doc)
  ~~|

:any/source/UnknownNameError/refusesANameNothingDeclares
  |~~ A name nothing declares is refused. ~~|
  ::law~(:nonexistent-binding | source !| [type /name] | eq [::any/source/UnknownNameError :nonexistent-binding])

:any/source/UnknownNameError/namesWhereTheWordLives
  |~~ A word's name is refused with the addresses where the word lives. ~~|
  ::law~(:filter | source !| /addresses | has ::vec/filter)

:any/source/UnknownNameError/name
  |~~ The name the axis read, a keyword or a tag. ~~|
  #[::keyword ::tag]

:any/source/UnknownNameError/addresses
  |~~ The addresses of the pages of that name, one for each kind that answers it. ~~|
  ::set

:any/source/UnknownNameError/nearest
  |~~ The names nearest to it in spelling. ~~|
  ::vec

:any/doc
  |~~ The page of a name, the passage from an address into its
      description: `::vec/filter | doc` the page of a verb by its
      address, `:name | doc` the page of the declaration that binds
      `:name` in its scope, null for a declaration without one. A page
      answers its text by `content`, its laws by `laws`, its links by
      `links` and its snippets by `snippets`. A keyword after `doc` is an
      anchor within the page of its subject, a vector of anchors a path,
      each read within the page the one before it reached, and a set of
      anchors several pages in one query.

      A name reads its own page.
      Any other value reads the page of its kind, and the `:kind` field
      of a map names nothing.
      A keyword reads the page of the member the subject holds under it,
      the word it calls or a part of the args of a word.
      A vector of anchors walks a path, and a set of anchors reads a page
      at each.
  ~~|
  ::builtin{}

:any/doc/readsThePageOfAVerb
  |~~ The page of `filter` speaks of its predicate. ~~|
  ::law~(::vec/filter | doc | content | contains "predicate")

:any/doc/readsThePageOfARefusal
  |~~ A refusal's page is a doc. ~~|
  ::law~(::number/add/SubjectNotNumberError | doc | type | eq ::doc)

:any/doc/readsThePageOfAKind
  |~~ A number reads the page of `::number`. ~~|
  ::law~(5 | doc | eq (::number | doc))

:any/doc/readsNoKindFromAField
  |~~ A map reads the page of maps, whatever its `:kind` says. ~~|
  ::law~({:kind ::set} | doc | eq (::map | doc))

:any/doc/readsAMemberByAnAnchor
  |~~ An anchor reads the page of a member. ~~|
  ::law~(::map | doc :minus | eq (::map/minus | doc))

:any/doc/readsTheWordAValueCalls
  |~~ An anchor on a value reads the page of the word it calls. ~~|
  ::law~([1 2] | doc :count | eq (::vec/count | doc))

:any/doc/readsAPartOfAWord
  |~~ An anchor reads the page of a part of a word. ~~|
  ::law~(:any/f ~(add n) | :any/f/n |~~ how many ~~| ~(/0) | ::any/f | doc :n | content | eq " how many ")

:any/doc/walksAPath
  |~~ A vector of anchors walks a path. ~~|
  ::law~(::vec | doc [:take] | eq (::vec/take | doc))

:any/doc/readsAPageAtEachAnchor
  |~~ A set of anchors reads a page at each. ~~|
  ::law~(::qlang | doc #[:pipeline :errors] | count | eq 2)

:any/doc/anchor
  |~~ The anchor within the page, null when it gives none. ~~|
  ~(at 0)

:any/doc/anchor/NotKeywordOrVecOrSetOrNullError
  |~~ The anchor is a keyword, a vector, a set or null. ~~|
  ::holds~(is ::keyword ::vec ::set ::null)

:any/doc/page
  |~~ The page of the name, null for a declaration without one. ~~|
  #[::doc ::null ::vec]

:any/doc/UnknownNameError
  |~~ `doc` found no declaration of the name it read, as `binding` finds
      none, or no member of the page before it under an anchor of its
      path.
      ::link~(::any/binding/UnknownNameError | doc)
  ~~|

:any/doc/UnknownNameError/refusesANameNothingDeclares
  |~~ A name nothing declares is refused. ~~|
  ::law~(:nonexistent-binding | doc !| [type /name] | eq [::any/doc/UnknownNameError :nonexistent-binding])

:any/doc/UnknownNameError/namesWhereTheWordLives
  |~~ A word's name is refused with the addresses where the word lives. ~~|
  ::law~(:filter | doc !| /addresses | has ::vec/filter)

:any/doc/UnknownNameError/namesWhereAnotherWordLives
  |~~ Another word's name is refused with its addresses. ~~|
  ::law~(:reduce | doc !| /addresses | has ::vec/reduce)

:any/doc/UnknownNameError/name
  |~~ The name the axis read, a keyword or a tag. ~~|
  #[::keyword ::tag]

:any/doc/UnknownNameError/addresses
  |~~ The addresses of the pages of that name, one for each kind that answers it. ~~|
  ::set

:any/doc/UnknownNameError/nearest
  |~~ The names nearest to it in spelling. ~~|
  ::vec

:any/doc/AnchorNotKeywordError
  |~~ An anchor of the path `doc` reads is no keyword: each step of the
      path names a member by a keyword, `doc [:take :count]`.
  ~~|

:any/doc/AnchorNotKeywordError/refusesANumberInThePath
  |~~ A number in the path is refused at its index. ~~|
  ::law~(::vec | doc [:take 1] !| [type /index] | eq [::any/doc/AnchorNotKeywordError 1])

:any/explain
  |~~ The explanation of the value, `::explanation{:page … :value …}`:
      the page `doc` reads for it, then the value itself, so it prints
      as the page above what it explains. A name reads its own
      page, a value the page of the kind it shows, and an error `!|`
      opened the page of its site, which says what failed.
  ~~|
  ::builtin{}

:any/explain/explainsAValueByItsKind
  |~~ A number is explained by the page of its kind. ~~|
  ::law~(42 | explain | page | eq (::number | doc))

:any/explain/explainsAWordByItsPage
  |~~ A word is explained by its page. ~~|
  ::law~(::number/add | explain | page | content | contains "Adds a number")

:any/explain/explainsAnErrorByItsSite
  |~~ An error is explained by the page of the word that refused. ~~|
  ::law~("x" | add 1 !| explain | page | content | contains "Adds a number")

:any/explain/explanation
  |~~ The page above the value it explains. ~~|
  ::explanation

:any/explain/UnknownNameError
  |~~ `explain` found no declaration of the page of its subject, as
      `binding` finds none.
      ::link~(::any/binding/UnknownNameError | doc)
  ~~|

:any/explain/UnknownNameError/refusesANameNothingDeclares
  |~~ A name nothing declares is refused. ~~|
  ::law~(:nonexistent-binding | explain !| [type /name] | eq [::any/explain/UnknownNameError :nonexistent-binding])

:any/explain/UnknownNameError/name
  |~~ The name the axis read, a keyword or a tag. ~~|
  #[::keyword ::tag]

:any/explain/UnknownNameError/addresses
  |~~ The addresses of the pages of that name, one for each kind that answers it. ~~|
  ::set

:any/explain/UnknownNameError/nearest
  |~~ The names nearest to it in spelling. ~~|
  ::vec

:any/laws
  |~~ The laws declared under the name the value reads, as `doc` reads
      it, in the order of their declarations: each one's name, which says
      the claim it proves, and the pair of its page, the sentence of the
      claim, and its query, which answers true. `runLaws` runs them. ~~|
  ::builtin{}

:any/laws/claims
  |~~ The name of each law and the pair of its page and its query. ~~|
  ::map

:any/laws/answersEachLawByItsName
  |~~ A law declared under a name is answered under its own name. ~~|
  ::law~(:any/k ~(5) | :any/k/answersFive |~~ k answers five. ~~| ::law~(0 | k | eq 5) | ::any/k | laws | keys | eq #[:answersFive])

:any/laws/pairsThePageWithTheQuery
  |~~ Each law is the pair of its page and its query. ~~|
  ::law~(:any/k ~(5) | :any/k/answersFive |~~ k answers five. ~~| ::law~(0 | k | eq 5) | ::any/k | laws | /answersFive | [(/0 | content) /1] | eq [" k answers five. " ~(0 | k | eq 5)])

:any/laws/UnknownNameError
  |~~ `laws` found no declaration of the name it read, as `binding`
      finds none.
      ::link~(::any/binding/UnknownNameError | doc)
  ~~|

:any/laws/UnknownNameError/name
  |~~ The name the axis read, a keyword or a tag. ~~|
  #[::keyword ::tag]

:any/laws/UnknownNameError/addresses
  |~~ The addresses of the pages of that name, one for each kind that answers it. ~~|
  ::set

:any/laws/UnknownNameError/nearest
  |~~ The names nearest to it in spelling. ~~|
  ::vec

:any/tag
  |~~ Lays a tag over a value, the name of the kind it is read as, the
      partner of the split `[type payload]`: a value is minted under a
      tag at run time as a literal `::Tag{…}` mints it where it is
      written. `value | tag ::Foo` tags its subject, `[tag value] | tag`
      reads the pair `[type payload]` answers, and `tag value tag` takes
      both, so `pair | tag /1 /0` reorders a pair into its place.

      A tag stands over the value, which its payload answers.
      A tagged value split into its type and payload tags back into
      itself.
      A tag over a tagged value nests it; replacing the tag goes through
      the payload, `tagged | payload | tag ::Other`.
      A tag laid over an error answers the error unchanged, since no tag
      stands over one; the fail track opens an error to its descriptor,
      which a tag and `raise` rename.
  ~~|
  ::builtin{}

:any/tag/standsOverTheValue
  |~~ The payload of a tagged number is the number. ~~|
  ::law~(42 | tag ::Box | payload | eq 42)

:any/tag/namesTheKind
  |~~ The tag is the type of the value. ~~|
  ::law~([1 2 3] | tag ::Triple | type | eq ::Triple)

:any/tag/tagsASplitBack
  |~~ A split value tags back into itself. ~~|
  ::law~(:Box |~~ A box. ~~| | ::Box[1 2 3] | [type payload] | tag | eq ::Box[1 2 3])

:any/tag/nestsATaggedValue
  |~~ A tag over a tagged value nests it. ~~|
  ::law~(:Inner |~~ An inner kind. ~~| | :Outer |~~ An outer kind. ~~| | ::Inner[1] | tag ::Outer | payload | type | eq ::Inner)

:any/tag/leavesAnErrorUnchanged
  |~~ A tag laid over an error answers the error. ~~|
  ::law~([::Box (!{:k 1})] | tag !| type | eq ::error)

:any/tag/raisesAnOpenedErrorAsItself
  |~~ An opened error split and tagged back raises as itself. ~~|
  ::law~(1 | add "1" !| [type payload] | tag | raise !| type | eq ::number/add/addend/NotNumberError)

:any/tag/name
  |~~ The name the call gives, null when it gives none. ~~|
  ~(at 0)

:any/tag/name/NotTagOrNullError
  |~~ The name is a tag name or null. ~~|
  ::holds~(is ::tag ::null)

:any/tag/PairTagNotTagError
  |~~ The tag of the pair `tag` reads is a tag name, `[::Box 5] | tag`; a
      keyword, `:box`, names a field and no kind.
  ~~|

:any/tag/PairTagNotTagError/refusesAKeyword
  |~~ A keyword names a field and no kind. ~~|
  ::law~([:foo 42] | tag !| [type /actualType] | eq [::any/tag/PairTagNotTagError ::keyword])

:any/tag/SubjectNotPairError
  |~~ `tag` alone reads a pair, `[::Box value] | tag`, the tag and the
      value it goes over; a subject of another shape is refused, a
      vector of another length among them.
  ~~|

:any/tag/SubjectNotPairError/refusesANumber
  |~~ A number is no pair. ~~|
  ::law~(42 | tag !| [type /actualType] | eq [::any/tag/SubjectNotPairError ::number])

:any/tag/SubjectNotPairError/refusesAVectorOfThree
  |~~ A vector of three is no pair. ~~|
  ::law~([::Foo 1 2] | tag !| [type /actualType] | eq [::any/tag/SubjectNotPairError ::vec])

:any/sumOf
  |~~ The sum of the numbers the call computes against the subject, as
      many as it gives, the subject only what they are read from: two
      fields of one map add by `sumOf /x /y`. ::call~(sumOf /x /y)
  ~~|
  ~(numbers | sum)

:any/sumOf/addsTwoFields
  |~~ Two fields of one map add. ~~|
  ::law~({:x 10 :y 3} | sumOf /x /y | eq 13)

:any/sumOf/addsAsManyAsGiven
  |~~ As many fields as the call gives add. ~~|
  ::law~({:a 1 :b 2 :c 3} | sumOf /a /b /c | eq 6)

:any/sumOf/numbers
  |~~ The numbers, one at least.
 ~~|
  ~(/)

:any/sumOf/numbers/EmptyError
  |~~ The numbers hold at least one value. ~~|
  ::holds~(count | gt 0)

:any/sumOf/numbers/ElementNotNumberError
  |~~ Each of the numbers is a number. ~~|
  ::holds~(every ~(is ::number))

:any/sumOf/sum
  |~~ The numbers added. ~~|
  ::number

:any/mulOf
  |~~ The product of the numbers the call computes against the subject,
      as many as it gives: the price and the quantity of one line multiply
      by `mulOf /price /qty`. ::call~(mulOf /price /qty)
  ~~|
  ~(factors | reduce 1 ~(mul))

:any/mulOf/multipliesTwoFields
  |~~ The price and the quantity multiply. ~~|
  ::law~({:price 4 :qty 3} | mulOf /price /qty | eq 12)

:any/mulOf/factors
  |~~ The numbers, one at least.
 ~~|
  ~(/)

:any/mulOf/factors/EmptyError
  |~~ The factors hold at least one value. ~~|
  ::holds~(count | gt 0)

:any/mulOf/factors/ElementNotNumberError
  |~~ Each of the factors is a number. ~~|
  ::holds~(every ~(is ::number))

:any/mulOf/product
  |~~ The numbers multiplied. ~~|
  ::number

:any/subOf
  |~~ The first number the call computes against the subject less the
      second: what is left to pay is `subOf /total /paid`.
      ::call~(subOf /total /paid)
  ~~|
  ~(minuend | sub subtrahend)

:any/subOf/subtractsTheSecond
  |~~ What is left to pay is the total less the paid. ~~|
  ::law~({:total 10 :paid 3} | subOf /total /paid | eq 7)

:any/subOf/minuend
  |~~ The number subtracted from. ~~|
  ~(/0)

:any/subOf/minuend/NotNumberError
  |~~ The minuend is a number. ~~|
  ::holds~(is ::number)
:any/subOf/subtrahend
  |~~ The number subtracted. ~~|
  ~(/1)

:any/subOf/subtrahend/NotNumberError
  |~~ The subtrahend is a number. ~~|
  ::holds~(is ::number)

:any/subOf/difference
  |~~ The first number less the second. ~~|
  ::number

:any/divOf
  |~~ The first number the call computes against the subject divided by
      the second: a mean is `divOf /sum /count`. ::call~(divOf /sum /count)
  ~~|
  ~(dividend | div divisor)

:any/divOf/dividesByTheSecond
  |~~ A mean is the sum over the count. ~~|
  ::law~({:sum 20 :count 4} | divOf /sum /count | eq 5)

:any/divOf/dividend
  |~~ The number divided. ~~|
  ~(/0)

:any/divOf/dividend/NotNumberError
  |~~ The dividend is a number. ~~|
  ::holds~(is ::number)
:any/divOf/divisor
  |~~ The number divided by. ~~|
  ~(/1)

:any/divOf/divisor/NotNumberError
  |~~ The divisor is a number. ~~|
  ::holds~(is ::number)

:any/divOf/quotient
  |~~ The first number divided by the second. ~~|
  ::number

:any/allOf
  |~~ Whether every condition the call computes against the subject holds:
      two conditions of one record hold together by `allOf /active (/age |
      gte 18)`. ::call~(allOf /active /verified)
  ~~|
  ~(conditions | every ~(/))

:any/allOf/holdsWhenEveryConditionHolds
  |~~ An active adult passes both conditions. ~~|
  ::law~({:active true :age 25} | allOf /active (/age | gte 18) | eq true)

:any/allOf/failsWhenOneFails
  |~~ A minor fails the second. ~~|
  ::law~({:active true :age 15} | allOf /active (/age | gte 18) | eq false)

:any/allOf/conditions
  |~~ The conditions, booleans, one at least.
 ~~|
  ~(/)

:any/allOf/conditions/EmptyError
  |~~ The conditions hold at least one value. ~~|
  ::holds~(count | gt 0)

:any/allOf/conditions/ElementNotBooleanError
  |~~ Each of the conditions is a boolean. ~~|
  ::holds~(every ~(is ::boolean))

:any/allOf/all
  |~~ Whether every condition holds. ~~|
  ::boolean

:any/anyOf
  |~~ Whether some condition the call computes against the subject holds:
      `anyOf /vip (/score | gt 95)`. ::call~(anyOf /vip /trial)
  ~~|
  ~(conditions | any ~(/))

:any/anyOf/holdsWhenOneHolds
  |~~ A high score passes without the flag. ~~|
  ::law~({:vip false :score 99} | anyOf /vip (/score | gt 95) | eq true)

:any/anyOf/conditions
  |~~ The conditions, booleans, one at least.
 ~~|
  ~(/)

:any/anyOf/conditions/EmptyError
  |~~ The conditions hold at least one value. ~~|
  ::holds~(count | gt 0)

:any/anyOf/conditions/ElementNotBooleanError
  |~~ Each of the conditions is a boolean. ~~|
  ::holds~(every ~(is ::boolean))

:any/anyOf/some
  |~~ Whether some condition holds. ~~|
  ::boolean

:any/unionOf
  |~~ The union of the maps or the sets the call computes against the
      subject, in their order, a later key holding over an earlier: the
      value of a pair with its name beside it is `unionOf /1 {:name (/0 |
      keyword)}`. ::call~(unionOf /a /b)
  ~~|
  ~(parts | drop 1 | reduce (parts | first) ~(union))

:any/unionOf/mergesTwoFields
  |~~ Two maps one value holds merge. ~~|
  ::law~({:a {:x 1} :b {:y 2}} | unionOf /a /b | eq {:x 1 :y 2})

:any/unionOf/foldsTheKeyIntoTheValue
  |~~ The key of a pair joins its value as a field. ~~|
  ::law~([:auth {:port 3000}] | unionOf /1 {:name (/0 | keyword)} | eq {:port 3000 :name "auth"})

:any/unionOf/parts
  |~~ The maps or the sets, one at least. ~~|
  ~(/)

:any/unionOf/parts/EmptyError
  |~~ The parts hold at least one value. ~~|
  ::holds~(count | gt 0)

:any/is
  |~~ Whether the walk of the subject's tags reaches a value of one of the
      kinds the call gives: a number under a tag of its own is a number, a
      set is a vector, a map under a tag is a map. A part of the args
      whose law asks `is` is handed to its name as the value of the kind
      the walk reached, and one whose law asks `every ~(is …)` as the
      vector, or the set, of each element's, a quote and a doc as they
      are, an element the walk refuses named by its index from zero.
      ::call~(is ::number)
  ~~|
  ::builtin{}

:any/is/holdsOfItsKind
  |~~ A number is a number. ~~|
  ::law~(5 | is ::number | eq true)

:any/is/walksUnderATag
  |~~ A number under a tag of its own is a number. ~~|
  ::law~(::Width(5) | is ::number | eq true)

:any/is/takesASetForAVector
  |~~ A set is a vector. ~~|
  ::law~(#[1 2] | is ::vec | eq true)

:any/is/failsOfOtherKinds
  |~~ A string is neither a number nor null. ~~|
  ::law~("5" | is ::number ::null | eq false)

:any/is/asksEachElementOfAPart
  |~~ The invariant of a part asks `is` of each element, a number under a tag among them. ~~|
  ::law~(:any/kindsOf ~(xs * type) | :any/kindsOf/xs |~~ X ~~| ~(/) | :any/kindsOf/xs/ElementNotNumberError ::holds~(every ~(is ::number)) | 5 | kindsOf ::W(1) 2 | eq [::number ::number])

:any/is/asksASetAsAVector
  |~~ A set given to a part is asked as its vector. ~~|
  ::law~(:any/setOf ~([(xs | type) (xs * type)]) | :any/setOf/xs |~~ X ~~| ~(/0) | :any/setOf/xs/ElementNotNumberError ::holds~(every ~(is ::number)) | 5 | setOf #[::W(1) 2] | eq [::set [::number ::number]])

:any/is/kinds
  |~~ The kinds, each a tag name; of none, no value is one. ~~|
  ~(/)

:any/is/kinds/refusesAKeyword
  |~~ A keyword is no kind, and is refused at its index. ~~|
  ::law~(5 | is :number !| [type /index /actualType] | eq [::any/is/kinds/ElementNotTagError 0 ::keyword])

:any/is/kinds/ElementNotTagError
  |~~ Each of the kinds is a tag name. ~~|
  ::holds~(every ~(is ::tag))

:any/is/reached
  |~~ Whether the walk of the tags reached one of the kinds. ~~|
  ::boolean
